Shift the inverse cipher's instructions by one halfword.

THIS IS AN EXPERIMENT, NOT A FIX, AND IT IS NOT PART OF ANY RESULT.  It
exists to test one claim and should be removed afterwards, or kept only
beside the capture it produced.

The claim.  This part's recovery saturates at 11 of 16 key bytes, and
the five it never gets are not under-sampled -- no usable byte pair
touches them, and their best pair sits at the noise floor while every
covered byte's best is 68-80.  What the five share is that four of them
are the table loads whose own instruction is the LAST HALFWORD OF AN
8-BYTE LINE, so the next instruction fetch must come from the next
line.  All four such loads are dark and none of the other twelve is,
except byte 0.  p = 0.003.  The proposed mechanism is that this cache is
unified, so the instruction line fetched in the load's shadow absorbs
its miss cost and the load stops changing the block time.

The test.  Move the instructions without changing them, so a DIFFERENT
four loads land at the end of a line, and see whether the dark set
moves with them.  A `nop` at the top of the function displaces
everything after it by two bytes, which is the smallest shift that
changes every offset mod 8.

Why not an alignment flag.  -falign-functions can only make an address
MORE aligned, so every shift it can produce is a multiple of 8 and
every load keeps its offset within the line.  The function already sits
at 0x0f40, which is 64-byte aligned, so those flags are no-ops here.
The shift has to come from inside.

Why a patch and not an edit.  aes.c compiles out of the pinned checkout,
which is never written to.  Until this experiment the build had no way
to patch mbedTLS at all -- the mechanism covered the SBSFU sources and
the Secure Engine and stopped there, which is the trap of a patch
mechanism that reaches half a vendor tree.  The Makefile now stages
mbedTLS the same way.

READ THE EMITTED ORDER AFTER APPLYING THIS.  A `nop` cannot change what
the function computes, but gcc may still schedule the round differently
around it, so which loads end a line has to be measured rather than
assumed:

  arm-none-eabi-objdump -d --no-show-raw-insn build/sbsfu.elf > build/sbsfu.dis
  python ../../results/bootloader_spi/l152_realistic/emitted_order.py \
      build/sbsfu.dis --isa thumb1 ...

If the same four loads still end a line, this patch has not performed
the experiment and the capture would prove nothing.

